## Stage 1 of the post-M5 hardening — the kept-cursor proof

### Mechanism (section 14.1 on the landed machinery)
- **`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`** (new, 1,302 lines): the 14.1.2 lift/lower driver. `export-task` validates the safepoint and the empty ready queue, captures the ordered waits without polling them, walks the reachable graph through `yin.vm.completion` with **one** source-resource-to-cell map shared by frames, waits, the store slice, closures and parked records, derives every dynamic reason and pending variant from the observed wait entry cross-checked against the static kinds (a variant the kinds do not admit refuses `:reason-mismatch`; `:call-effect` is never invented), carries each live install child as its own whole handoff body (a name alone refuses `:incomplete-install`; obligations the child will bind are discharged, not refused), closes the requirements, validates the body, and answers it as canonical CBOR bytes under their content address. `resume-task` checks tag/version/stamp/grammar/cell-exactness/code hashes before anything is installed, attaches each stream identity once through its carried descriptor, allocates fresh private resources per cell, re-seals references under the receiver, restores the isolated store (keys through `engine/store-put`, so a reserved key in a tampered body refuses), module stores, counter, parked records, installs and ordered waits, and answers the machine only after all restoration passes. Halt is a result with no frame; an explicit park is the no-wait shape.
- **`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`**: the section-8 owner ruling is now executable — `:yin.safepoint/reasons` is replaced by `:yin.safepoint/kinds` (`ucf/parking-kinds`: `[:explicit-park] [:blocked-read] [:blocked-write] [:ffi-sent :ffi-retained] [:effectful-call]`); pins migrated in `ucf_test.cljc`.
- **`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`**: the lower's isolated-store install joined the allowlist with its reason.

### Tests (`test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_test.cljc`, 17 deftests / 107 assertions)
All of 14.1.3's rows over real machines parked through the engine, moving actual canonical bytes through the served-table/remote-table seam: aliasing (shared cell in two ordered waiters, a closure and the store, plus two independents at one position; `["B" "C" "B" "B"]` in wait order), every safepoint kind with parity against the source's own resumed run (blocked read at two depths, blocked write, sent and retained FFI, retained and polling link waits, an install waiter whose child the receiver steps to completion, explicit park, halt-as-result), interleaved/duplicate/late responses around the correlated one with no reissue and future calls on the receiver's pair, eviction (source's own gap and exact successor cursor), route loss (`:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming the stream), profile omission refused before publication, and the refusal battery (not-quiescent, not-at-safepoint, reason mismatch, forged resource reference, missing/extra/malformed cells, malformed frames, code hash mismatch, wrong version, non-body bytes).

**Matrix coverage:** the three same-host pairs (JVM→JVM, Node→Node, Dart→Dart) run as these tests on their own lanes — the handoff deftests were confirmed executed and passing on all three. The six cross-host pairs (JVM↔Node, JVM↔Dart, Node↔Dart) are **not** landed: they need a handoff peer program per host and process transport. That gap is recorded — not inferred from same-host bytes, per 14.1.3's "process transport coverage is recorded separately" — in the test namespace docstring and in the design record; the pattern to build on exists (`yin.repl.slice-peer` + the spawn tests).

### UCF amendment record
`docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`: section 5 gains I-5 (the kinds migration and the handoff driver; no stamp component changed, so no image validity changes); section 6 records the six obligations the stage-2 `:yin.k/version 1` amendment must carry (the handoff body envelope as wire fact, the explicit-park no-wait shape, install-child completeness, the dynamic-reason rule, cell identity discipline, the cursor-profile claim).

